OpenWrt Podman maintenance: /usr/bin/podman-stack {watchdog|update}

One trusted shell definition per container, /etc/podman-stack.d/*.conf:

    NAME=SmartDNS
    IMAGE=docker.io/pymumu/smartdns:latest
    create() {
        podman create --name "$NAME" \
            --network=host --restart=always --cap-add=NET_RAW \
            --volume smartdns-conf:/etc/smartdns \
            "$IMAGE"
    }

Definitions are sourced as root: only install trusted files, and specify every
required creation option, including mounts, network, health check and labels.
The create function must create NAME from IMAGE. Update skips missing and
--rm containers and exits nonzero for those definitions. No definition is
required by the watchdog.

Watchdog runs health probes for running containers. On unhealthy it uses the
Podman 5.8 service-stop option and starts the container again. Failed starts
are retried on later runs using a temporary marker keyed by container ID.
A stopped container with restart policy always or unless-stopped is also
started, unless Podman reports StoppedByUser=true. Newly created containers,
on-failure retry policies and containers without restart policy are not
started automatically, except when retrying the watchdog's own failed restart.
An explicit podman stop sets StoppedByUser and cancels a pending retry. The
service-stop option is a hidden Podman CLI option and must be verified on the
installed version. If unsupported, the failed stop is logged and no fallback
stop is issued. Podman state and a concurrent manual stop can race; do not
perform simultaneous manual and watchdog operations on the same container.
Retry markers live in /var/run and do not survive a reboot; after reboot the
container's own restart policy and the LuCI procd service determine recovery.

Updates pull first, stop the old container if needed, rename it to
NAME-podman-stack-backup, create and (if previously running) start the new one,
then remove the backup. If creation or start fails, rollback removes the new
container and restores the old one. A successful start is not a health or
readiness check. The update exits nonzero on errors and does not globally prune
images. It tags the immediately preceding image under
localhost/podman-stack-backup/ and records its ID in .state. On the next successful update it removes its own older tag and
tries to delete that older image only if it has no other tags; Podman refuses
removal while a container still uses it. Keep the reserved backup tags and state
together. Interrupted cleanup or images with other users may leave extra images.
This retains an image for manual recovery; it does not automatically roll back
when an application fails later.
A process interruption, power loss, failed rollback or failed backup removal
can leave the backup behind; future updates refuse to overwrite it. Inspect both
containers and resolve them manually before retrying. Persistent volumes are not
rolled back: application migrations may have already changed data.

Watchdog and update share a lock; this does not lock LuCI or other Podman
commands. Avoid running the LuCI image updater or manually modifying containers
at the same time. LuCI reconstructs settings from inspect and does not preserve
all options; it is not a substitute for these explicit definitions. Logs and
command errors go to logread under the tag podman-stack. Health probe failures
are expected and not logged individually; actual unhealthy restarts are logged.
The lock lives under /var/run; if interrupted before its pid is written, remove
/var/run/podman-stack.lock only after confirming no instance is active.
